<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tyana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tuwanuwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tyana was an ancient city of southern Cappadocia in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, at modern Kemerhisar near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigde">Niğde</a>. Known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> as Tuwanuwa, it was the capital of the Neo-Hittite kingdom of Tuwana in the 8th century BCE. In Roman times it was a prosperous city on the road to the Cilician Gates, birthplace of the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-tyana">Apollonius of Tyana</a>, and remains of its Roman aqueduct still stand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tyana.jpg" alt="Tyana" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tyana,_Cappadocia,_Turkey_(23777549058).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tyana lay in Cappadocia, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> near the Cilician Gates, and the district around it was known in Classical Antiquity as Tyanitis. The name came down through several languages: Tūwanuwa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>, Tuwana in Luwian, Tuḫana in Neo-Assyrian Akkadian and Tyana in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. According to later Hittite sources the city was an important cult centre, whose local gods were the Storm-god Tarḫunzas of Tūwanuwa and his consort Šaḫḫaššara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tūwanuwa first appears in Hittite texts. The Telipinu Proclamation lists it among the territories conquered by Labarna I, the 17th-century BCE founder of the Old Kingdom. Under Tudhaliya III it became a border town after Arzawa invaded the Lower Land, and the prince Suppiluliuma recaptured it and used it as a base to reconquer the region. After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> it became the centre of the Luwian-speaking kingdom of Tuwana, the largest and most prominent of the southern Tabalian states. Its kings, probably one dynasty, included Warpalawas II, who offered tribute to the Neo-Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> in 738 and 737 BCE. Sargon II later placed Tuwana under an Assyrian governor.</p><p>Tyana was an important Persian administrative centre, mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it was renamed Eusebeia near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a>, probably after Ariarathes V Eusebes refounded it, and was, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Mazaca</a>, the second most important city in Cappadocia. Under Roman rule it was among the most Hellenised cities of the region and was the reputed birthplace of the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-tyana">Apollonius of Tyana</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> it became a colony, and the emperor Aurelian took it in 272 after it had sided with Shapur and then with Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>. In 372 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> made it capital of Cappadocia Secunda.</p><p>After the Muslim conquests the Byzantine frontier ran along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a>, and Tyana was raided in 708, 806 and 831. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> sacked it in 708 after a long siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> took it in 806 and began to make it a military base, and Al-Abbas ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> razed it in 831 and rebuilt it three years later, until the campaign was abandoned after al-Ma'mun's death in 833. It declined after 933. Tyana was a bishopric from 325 and a metropolitan see until the fourteenth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site corresponds to the modern town of Kemerhisar in Niğde Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Ruins of the Byzantine city are still visible, and in 2020 excavations uncovered an octagonal church and coins of the fourth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tyana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It emerged as a separate kingdom in the 8th century BCE around the old city of <b>Tuwanuwa</b>, later Greek Tyana.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuwana">Tuwana</a></li></ul></section>
